 ## Background
-The concept of using deformation fields to handle non-rigid transformations in NeRFs was introduced by Park et al. in November 2020 with the paper "Nerfies" [S1]. This work strongly improved model performance in dynamic scenes where baseline NeRFs failed. Concurrently, NerFace (December 2020) used per-frame learned latent codes with facial expression coefficients [S1]. Subsequently, HyperNeRF (June 2021) built upon Nerfies by extending the canonical space approach [S1].
+Deformation field modeling originated with the introduction of Nerfies by Park et al. in November 2020, which applied deformation fields to handle non-rigid transformations in dynamic scenes [S2]. Concurrently, D-NeRF introduced similar canonical space mappings for dynamic radiance fields [S1]. The approach has since been adapted to 3D Gaussian Splatting, with methods like Dynamic 3D Gaussian Splatting and SWAGS extending the concept to explicit geometric primitives [S1]. Applications include medical imaging, such as markless motion reconstruction for infants [S1], and monocular endoscopic reconstruction [S1].
 
